                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the deviant behaviour`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `You have segmented data on four non-compliance types, journey maps, complaint transcripts and the enforcement-cost ledger. Before designing anything, apply Dootson’s deviance lens: for your target behaviour, separate the <strong>can’t-comply</strong> (financial hardship, friction, confusion) from the <strong>won’t-comply</strong> (deliberate, opportunistic defiance) using <em>Capability, Opportunity, Motivation</em> (COM-B). The two need opposite interventions, and <em>misdiagnosing them is the costliest error.</em> Non-payment is the biggest dollar problem — A$47M tied up across ~154,000 accounts, mostly people who want to pay and can’t. Size the problem, then pick the behaviour where a nudge can move the needle fastest.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Design the intervention: human or digital`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Design the nudge for your target behaviour. Choose a <strong>choice-architecture mechanism</strong> — social-norm messaging, an opt-out default, friction reduction, salience and timing, or a commitment device — and the <strong>channel</strong>: human (frontline scripts, proactive calls, community ambassadors) or digital (app prompts, smart-meter alerts). Channel must match the audience: much of the can’t-comply core is over 70 or has no smartphone, so a digital-only nudge reaches almost no one who matters. Above all, run the <em>ethics check</em> — transparency, equity, consent — and avoid disguised coercion: a “last warning” countdown is the enforcement that triggered the inquiry, in a smaller font.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Set the rollout scope and test design`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Decide how far and how fast within the A$3M and the 90 days. A tightly controlled <strong>randomised pilot</strong> or a <strong>multi-arm human-vs-digital test</strong> buys you defensible evidence; a single-suburb trial is a quick read; a fast broad rollout buys speed and loses containment. Design the measurement: a <em>control group</em> is the counterfactual that separates your nudge from the heatwave breaking. The advocacy group’s class complaint and the interim report are pushing you to rush — but a 6% rise with no control proves nothing to the regulator. And decide where <em>proportionate enforcement</em> still belongs for the deliberate won’t-comply.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Read the results and scale responsibly`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The pilot results are in — and they are heterogeneous. The social-norm message lifted payment in the mainstream but <em>increased</em> breaches among low-volume users who learned they were below average: the <strong>boomerang effect</strong>. Human channels outperformed digital on the can’t-comply core; some nudges did nothing on the won’t-comply. Read the heterogeneity, not just the average. Decide what to <strong>scale</strong>, what to <strong>redesign</strong>, and what to <strong>stop</strong> — then present to the regulator a behaviourally grounded, ethical compliance strategy with the counterfactual, the effect by segment, and the guardrails that keep it the right side of the line.`,

