                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_payment:       `<strong>Sensible target.</strong> Non-payment is the biggest dollar problem — A$47M across ~154,000 accounts — and most of it is can’t-comply, people who want to pay and hit a wall. That is exactly where an enabling nudge can move the needle fast. Now make sure your diagnosis splits the genuine hardship from the small deliberate core.`,
                    coach_r1_restriction:   `<strong>You’re chasing the headline.</strong> Restriction breaches are what went viral, but the segment is a mix of can’t and won’t — harder to move cleanly with one nudge. It’s a defensible target if your diagnosis is sharp; just don’t let the media cycle pick your behaviour for you.`,
                    coach_r1_fraud:         `<strong>You’ve aimed your first pilot at the deliberate cheats.</strong> Hardship-claim fraud is the won’t-comply core — opportunistic, motivated to defy. An enabling nudge backfires here, and “punish the worst hardest” is the enforcement reflex that opened the inquiry. Nudges aren’t your tool for this segment; proportionate enforcement is.`,
                    coach_r1_all:           `<strong>One big campaign for four different behaviours is the one-size error.</strong> You’d send the same message to a hardship pensioner and a deliberate non-payer — shaming one, ignored by the other. One message, four opposite failures. Pick a behaviour and diagnose it before you design.`,
                    coach_r1_split:         `<strong>That’s the move that precedes design.</strong> Splitting can’t-comply from won’t-comply with COM-B tells you the lever: enable capability and opportunity for the hardship core, and reserve a different tool for the deliberate. Misdiagnosing the two is the single most expensive mistake in this case — you didn’t make it.`,
                    coach_r1_onebucket:     `<strong>“Non-compliance is non-compliance” collapses two opposite problems into one.</strong> The pensioner who can’t pay and the operator who won’t need opposite interventions — an enabling nudge for one, legitimate enforcement for the other. One bucket guarantees you get at least one of them wrong.`,
                    coach_r1_moral:         `<strong>“They just won’t do the right thing” is the framing that caused the crisis.</strong> Dootson’s whole point is that deviant behaviour has identifiable drivers — hardship, friction, confusion — not moral failure to be punished. Reach for consequences first and you reproduce the inquiry, in a nudge-shaped wrapper.`,
                    coach_r1_enforcelens:   `<strong>Targeting by who you fined most just re-runs enforcement.</strong> The fine ledger tells you who you punished, not why they broke the rule. Anchor the diagnosis in segmented behaviour, journey maps and the complaint transcripts — the deviance lens, not the debt-collection one.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> A movable target, a genuine can’t-vs-won’t split, and an evidence base built on COM-B and the customer journey. That’s the spine the rest of the pilot hangs on — design now has somewhere defensible to start.`,
                    coach_r2_enabling:      `<strong>An enabling bundle fits the can’t-comply core.</strong> Opt-out plans, one-tap hardship registration, removed friction and timely reminders make the compliant choice the easy choice — Thaler-and-Sunstein choice architecture, not pressure. For people who want to comply and can’t, this is the highest-leverage design you have.`,
                    coach_r2_socialnorm:    `<strong>Social norms lift the mainstream — and carry a boomerang.</strong> “9 in 10 neighbours complied” pulls the above-average down toward the norm. Powerful for the broad middle, risky for low-volume users who learn they’re already below average. Note that risk now; you’ll have to read for it in Round 4.`,
                    coach_r2_threat:        `<strong>A red-bordered countdown to disconnection is not a nudge.</strong> It’s the enforcement that just triggered a regulator inquiry and a viral backlash — in a smaller font. Nothing about it changes the dynamic that put you in crisis. This is the line the whole case turns on, and you’ve crossed it.`,
                    coach_r2_commitment:    `<strong>A pledge with no support behind it asks the can’t-comply to promise the impossible.</strong> Commitment devices work when the barrier is motivation, not capability — for a hardship customer, “pledge to save” without enabling the saving just sets them up to fail and feel worse.`,
                    coach_r2_channelmiss:   `<strong>Elegant app prompt, wrong audience.</strong> Much of your can’t-comply core is over 70 or has no smartphone — a digital-only nudge reaches almost none of the people who matter, so adoption craters where it counts. Is this a digital problem, or a human-channel one?`,
                    coach_r2_clean:         `<strong>The right mechanism, the right channel, and a real ethics check.</strong> An enabling bundle delivered through a blended, audience-matched channel, with transparency, an equity pass and genuine consent. That is a legitimate nudge — and it’s defensible to the regulator and the advocacy panel both.`,
                    coach_r2_disguise:      `<strong>Softer wording on the same pressure is still coercion.</strong> Making the threat sound friendlier doesn’t move it the right side of the line — the customer still feels the pressure, and the advocacy group still has its class complaint. Transparency means the intent is honest, not just the tone.`,
                    coach_r2_skipethics:    `<strong>“A nudge can’t cause harm” is exactly how a nudge causes harm.</strong> Shaming the wrong segment, excluding the vulnerable, eroding consent — these are the equity and manipulation risks the inquiry is about. Skip the ethics pass and you’re one screenshot from the next viral backlash.`,
                    coach_r2_gold:          `<strong>You gold-plated every channel before proving anything works.</strong> A$1.4M of the A$3M pilot is committed up front — that’s spend ahead of evidence, and it tightens every later choice. Design well, but keep budget for the test that actually earns the scale-up.`,
                    coach_r3_pilot:         `<strong>Rigour first.</strong> A tightly controlled randomised pilot on the target segment is exactly what gives the regulator a number it can trust. You trade some speed and coverage for evidence that survives scrutiny — the right trade when an inquiry is watching.`,
                    coach_r3_multiarm:      `<strong>A multi-arm test answers the question you most need answered.</strong> Randomised human-vs-digital arms tell you which channel actually moves the can’t-comply core — the channel-fit question Round 2 raised. It costs more and takes discipline, but it’s how you learn what to scale.`,
                    coach_r3_suburb:        `<strong>A single-suburb trial is a quick read, not strong evidence.</strong> One postcode gives you a feel and limited coverage, but without randomisation the regulator can still ask what else differs about that suburb. Useful as a step; thin as a counterfactual.`,
                    coach_r3_broad:         `<strong>Fast and wide buys speed and loses containment.</strong> Push to all 1.4M now and you can’t isolate the nudge, can’t contain a backfire, and can’t defend the number. Speed feels like progress to the board; to the regulator it reads as the same un-evidenced reflex as the fines.`,
                    coach_r3_nocontrol:     `<strong>No control group, no counterfactual.</strong> If compliance rises 6% you won’t be able to tell the nudge from the heatwave breaking — and the regulator <em>will</em> ask. Without a control you have a story, not evidence, and a story is what got the last approach in trouble.`,
                    coach_r3_weakcontrol:   `<strong>A loose comparison suburb is better than nothing — but only just.</strong> It isn’t randomised, so the regulator can still attribute the change to something else about that area. If you can afford the discipline, a randomised control is what makes the result defensible.`,
                    coach_r3_dropall:       `<strong>Dropping all enforcement over-corrects.</strong> Nudges and enforcement are complements, not substitutes — strip every consequence and the deliberate won’t-comply free-ride harder once they see no one’s watching. Behaviour change covers the can’t; proportionate enforcement still covers the won’t.`,
                    coach_r3_punishall:     `<strong>Fining everyone with a nudge bolted on top is enforcement in disguise.</strong> If the consequence regime is unchanged, the “nudge” is PR — and the advocacy group will say so. You can’t reform the approach while keeping the very practice the inquiry condemned.`,
                    coach_r4_hetero:        `<strong>You read the heterogeneity, not the headline.</strong> Separating the effect by segment and channel — what worked, what did nothing, what boomeranged — is exactly what lets you scale responsibly instead of scaling a backfire to 1.4 million people.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>“Small enough to ignore” is how a backfire becomes a scandal.</strong> The boomerang sub-segment is still real people whose breaches you increased. Discount it now and you’ll either scale the harm or have the advocacy group surface it for you.`,
                    coach_r4_average:       `<strong>The average hides the damage.</strong> Compliance rose overall, but inside that number a sub-segment got worse. Read only the mean and you’ll scale the social-norm nudge to the very users it boomeranged on — the error this round is built to catch.`,
                    coach_r4_selective:     `<strong>Scale what worked, redesign what backfired, stop what failed.</strong> That’s responsible scaling — you keep the enabling bundle and the channel that moved the can’t-comply, fix the boomerang for low-volume users, and drop what did nothing. The regulator can see you read your own evidence.`,
                    coach_r4_scaleall:      `<strong>You’re about to scale a nudge that backfired to 1.4 million people.</strong> The social-norm message increased breaches among low-volume users — push it state-wide and you multiply that harm and hand the advocacy group its case. Which result did you mean to scale?`,
                    coach_r4_stopall:       `<strong>Stopping everything throws out the evidence you built.</strong> One sub-segment boomeranged; the enabling bundle still moved the can’t-comply core. Abandoning the whole programme back to enforcement is the over-correction — you’re binning what worked to avoid redesigning what didn’t.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That’s the strategy the regulator can accept.</strong> A counterfactual that isolates the nudge, the effect read by segment, honest ethics guardrails, and a clear scale/redesign/stop call. You replaced punitive enforcement with something tested, ethical and defensible — the brief, delivered.`,
                    coach_r4_spin:          `<strong>Leading with the one good number is how the regulator stops trusting you.</strong> They can see the whole pilot. Spin the favourable metric and bury the rest, and they’ll assume the rest is worse than it is — and impose their own remedy anyway.`,
                    coach_r4_reskin:        `<strong>Rebranding a crackdown as “behavioural” is the trap the whole case warns against.</strong> Promising tougher enforcement in nudge language is disguised coercion — the exact move that, if you re-skin it, makes the regulator’s remedy mandatory and permanent.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
