                    sum_r3_punish:          `Kept fining everyone — enforcement in disguise.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Test design partially rigorous.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Read the heterogeneity; scaled selectively; evidence-led pitch.`,
                    sum_r4_backfire:        `Scaled a backfired nudge state-wide.`,
                    sum_r4_stop:            `Abandoned the programme back to enforcement.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Read or scaled the results loosely.`,

                    insight_behavioural:    `Behavioural strategy proven: you diagnosed can’t-comply from won’t-comply, built an enabling nudge through a channel matched to the audience, tested it against a real control group within budget, and scaled responsibly. The regulator has a counterfactual-backed compliance uplift, ethical guardrails it can verify, and a strategy that replaces punitive enforcement — the brief, delivered.`,
                    insight_coercion:       `Coercion trap: you branded enforcement as a nudge — a countdown threat, softened pressure, or a rebranded crackdown. That reproduces the exact dynamic that triggered the inquiry and the viral backlash. No compliance number can rescue a “nudge” the advocacy group can screenshot as a threat; the regulator’s remedy becomes mandatory.`,
                    insight_nocontrol:      `No counterfactual: you measured a rise without a control group, so you cannot tell the nudge from the heatwave breaking. The regulator asked for evidence and you brought a story — which is what got the last approach condemned. The uplift is uncredible at scale.`,
                    insight_channel:        `Channel mismatch: a digital-only nudge aimed at a largely over-70, low-smartphone can’t-comply core drove adoption to near zero where it mattered most. The design was elegant; it just never reached the people whose behaviour you needed to move.`,
                    insight_onesize:        `Misdiagnosis: you either treated four different behaviours as one or aimed an enabling nudge at the deliberate won’t-comply. The same message shames the can’t-comply and bounces off the won’t — the costliest error in the case, and the one diagnosis was supposed to prevent.`,
                    insight_backfire:       `Backfire scaled: a nudge that lifted the average while boomeranging on a sub-segment was pushed state-wide, multiplying the harm to the very users it hurt. Reading the mean instead of the heterogeneity is how a pilot success becomes a population-scale failure.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed pilot: parts of the diagnosis, design, test and scale-up landed, but they didn’t fully reinforce each other — enough gaps that the regulator can still question whether behaviour, not the weather, moved the number.`,
                    insight_nodiagnosis:    `You never ran a genuine can’t-vs-won’t diagnosis: without COM-B and the deviance lens, every later choice was a guess about why customers misbehave — and the regulator can see the design had no defensible starting point.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You overspent the capped A$3M pilot: cost discipline is part of the credibility test, and a behavioural approach that blows its budget hands the board a reason to revert to the cheaper enforcement reflex.`,
                    insight_standing_low:   `Regulator and trust standing stayed low: even where the design had merit, the way you scoped, tested or communicated it left the regulator unconvinced and the advocacy group unsatisfied.`,
                    insight_evidence:       `Your evidence holds: a counterfactual-backed uplift, a behaviour-change index built on diagnosis-fit and ethics, and adoption among the segment that mattered. This is the difference between a slogan and a tested intervention — and it is what earns the state-wide scale-up.`,

                    hint_coercion:          `You’ve crossed the line into disguised coercion — the exact dynamic that caused the crisis. A real nudge changes the choice architecture without removing choice or relying on threat. Pivot to an enabling, transparent design before the regulator’s report lands.`,
                    hint_channel:           `Your channel doesn’t fit the audience — adoption is near zero where it matters. Much of the can’t-comply core is low-digital; bring in a human channel for them and reserve digital for the reachable rest.`,
                    hint_onesize:           `You’re running a one-size diagnosis — the same intervention can’t fit can’t-comply and won’t-comply. Split the segment with COM-B, enable the hardship core, and reserve proportionate enforcement for the deliberate.`,
                    hint_nocontrol:         `You have no counterfactual — the regulator won’t accept an uplift you can’t separate from the weather. Add a randomised control group and hold rollout discipline before you claim a result.`,
                    hint_standing:          `Regulator and trust standing is dangerously low. Lead with transparency, an equity pass and defensible evidence — and stay clear of anything that reads as the old enforcement in nudge clothing.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — a credible uplift, a high behaviour-change index and the regulator’s trust. Land it: read the heterogeneity, scale selectively, and pitch the evidence, not a spin.`,

